<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satakarni I</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Satakarni I was an early Satavahana king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, successor of Kanha, generally placed in the second or first century BCE. He extended the kingdom over the western Deccan and probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, and performed Vedic horse sacrifices recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naneghat">Naneghat</a> inscription of his queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naganika">Naganika</a>. He may be the Satakarni named as a rival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharavela">Kharavela</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathigumpha-inscription">Hathigumpha inscription</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/satakarni-i.jpg" alt="Satakarni I" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Ancientcoinsofindia1, English Wikipedia · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Satkarni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
